It's about who I'm naturally curious to learn from,

1837
01:13:19.830 --> 01:13:23.610
who I feel a gravitational pull towards.

1838
01:13:23.610 --> 01:13:27.840
And that's really been the guiding principle or light.

1839
01:13:27.840 --> 01:13:29.520
And it's all about curiosity.

1840
01:13:29.520 --> 01:13:31.020
Love it.

1841
01:13:31.020 --> 01:13:33.720
Second thing, listen, Adam,

1842
01:13:33.720 --> 01:13:36.510
you need to hear this, preparation.

1843
01:13:36.510 --> 01:13:37.500
Oh.

1844
01:13:37.500 --> 01:13:39.210
Preparation.

1845
01:13:39.210 --> 01:13:42.780
There are no shortcuts to anything.

1846
01:13:42.780 --> 01:13:45.270
You can't cram for a competition.

1847
01:13:45.270 --> 01:13:50.200
You can't fake readiness, in order to be present,

1848
01:13:51.420 --> 01:13:54.990
in order to be able to execute to the best of your ability,

1849
01:13:54.990 --> 01:13:58.980
I cannot overemphasize the importance of preparation.

1850
01:13:58.980 --> 01:14:02.913
And in the context of this podcast, I would say on average,

1851
01:14:04.500 --> 01:14:09.500
maybe between six to eight hours goes into each guest.

1852
01:14:10.590 --> 01:14:12.810
Reading their books, listening to other podcasts

1853
01:14:12.810 --> 01:14:15.780
they've been on, reading all the material that I can find.

1854
01:14:15.780 --> 01:14:19.710
Background material, news articles, blog posts, et cetera,

1855
01:14:19.710 --> 01:14:22.110
and then synthesizing all of that material,

1856
01:14:22.110 --> 01:14:23.310
coming up with an outline.

1857
01:14:23.310 --> 01:14:27.630
Like I probably go way too far on the preparation thing,

1858
01:14:27.630 --> 01:14:30.990
but it's that thing, if you're an actor,

1859
01:14:30.990 --> 01:14:32.970
you can't just memorize the lines,

1860
01:14:32.970 --> 01:14:35.400
they have to become so rote that you forget them.

1861
01:14:35.400 --> 01:14:38.580
And you can show up and be present for the performance.

1862
01:14:38.580 --> 01:14:41.310
Similarly, I have to digest all of this

1863
01:14:41.310 --> 01:14:45.000
and allow it to kind of percolate in my unconscious mind

1864
01:14:45.000 --> 01:14:48.270
so that when I show up to do the podcast,

1865
01:14:48.270 --> 01:14:50.220
I'm not reliant upon looking on notes

1866
01:14:50.220 --> 01:14:52.320
even though I have the iPad open in case

1867
01:14:52.320 --> 01:14:54.000
I have a brain fart or something that.

1868
01:14:54.000 --> 01:14:57.810
And I just trust in the preparation and try to be as present

1869
01:14:57.810 --> 01:15:01.470
as possible and allow it to be what it wants to be.

1870
01:15:01.470 --> 01:15:06.470
But I think what you see are a lot of people faking

1871
01:15:06.720 --> 01:15:09.783
the preparation part and trying to kind of,

1872
01:15:11.760 --> 01:15:14.470
get through certain experiences and play it off

1873
01:15:17.520 --> 01:15:18.930
as if they had prepared.

1874
01:15:18.930 --> 01:15:20.853
But you can always tell.

1875
01:15:21.870 --> 01:15:22.703
Yeah, 100%.

1876
01:15:22.703 --> 01:15:23.761
I mean, I'm all about-

1877
01:15:23.761 --> 01:15:25.170
It's a short term versus long term thing.

1878
01:15:25.170 --> 01:15:26.003
Sorry, go ahead.

1879
01:15:26.003 --> 01:15:29.160
No, yeah, I'm all about the over researching

1880
01:15:29.160 --> 01:15:30.210
when I'm writing something,

1881
01:15:30.210 --> 01:15:33.180
I always over research, talk to too many people.

1882
01:15:33.180 --> 01:15:35.370
I have too many people's quotes that

1883
01:15:35.370 --> 01:15:37.950
I wanna get in there that I can't always do.

1884
01:15:37.950 --> 01:15:40.080
You feel bad about it sometimes

1885
01:15:40.080 --> 01:15:41.370
because you can't get to everything,

1886
01:15:41.370 --> 01:15:42.960
but so it creates this other problem,

1887
01:15:42.960 --> 01:15:45.090
but in the end it always delivers better material.

1888
01:15:45.090 --> 01:15:49.390
So I think in media, preparation is key, six to eight hours

1889
01:15:50.820 --> 01:15:52.473
'cause it seems you put in,

1890
01:15:53.910 --> 01:15:55.770
I wouldn't have been surprised if it was 30 hours

1891
01:15:55.770 --> 01:15:57.928
based on some of the interviews you get into.

1892
01:15:57.928 --> 01:15:59.550
Yeah, I mean, and it depends,

1893
01:15:59.550 --> 01:16:03.510
it depends on, with authors it's generally more.

1894
01:16:03.510 --> 01:16:05.580
'Cause you have to consume their books and all of that.

1895
01:16:05.580 --> 01:16:10.580
But and this is applicable to everything.

1896
01:16:11.220 --> 01:16:13.950
Like if you're gonna be an athlete,

1897
01:16:13.950 --> 01:16:16.590
you wanna show up for your competition prepared.

1898
01:16:16.590 --> 01:16:20.460
Like you don't wanna be trying to get away with something

1899
01:16:20.460 --> 01:16:21.960
that you actually didn't earn.

1900
01:16:21.960 --> 01:16:24.750
And all the things that you seek through

1901
01:16:24.750 --> 01:16:26.583
participating in a competition,

1902
01:16:28.766 --> 01:16:32.310
the enhanced sense of self and pride and all of that,

1903
01:16:32.310 --> 01:16:36.660
are really a function of preparedness.

1904
01:16:36.660 --> 01:16:39.761
Like if you didn't really put the preparation in,

1905
01:16:39.761 --> 01:16:40.800
and you kind of get away with it,

1906
01:16:40.800 --> 01:16:42.690
even if you get the good result,

1907
01:16:42.690 --> 01:16:44.940
you're not getting that feeling.

1908
01:16:44.940 --> 01:16:47.670
You're not getting that enriched experience

1909
01:16:47.670 --> 01:16:50.430
that you would have had you put yourself on the line

1910
01:16:50.430 --> 01:16:51.480
and tried your hardest.

1911
01:16:51.480 --> 01:16:54.900
Which means investing in that preparation piece.

1912
01:16:54.900 --> 01:16:55.750
Love it.

1913
01:16:56.700 --> 01:16:58.683
Next thing, listening.

1914
01:17:00.210 --> 01:17:03.840
Most people are terrible listeners.

1915
01:17:03.840 --> 01:17:07.770
If you can master listening, the art of listening,

1916
01:17:07.770 --> 01:17:10.170
you're already gonna be way ahead in just about

1917
01:17:10.170 --> 01:17:11.940
everything important in life.

1918
01:17:11.940 --> 01:17:13.680
I think that's true.

1919
01:17:13.680 --> 01:17:14.880
I'm sorry, what'd you say?

1920
01:17:14.880 --> 01:17:16.650
See, I know.

1921
01:17:16.650 --> 01:17:18.335
This is why Roll On never works.

1922
01:17:18.335 --> 01:17:19.650
(Adam laughs)

1923
01:17:19.650 --> 01:17:20.483
Right?

1924
01:17:21.540 --> 01:17:22.980
I agree.

1925
01:17:22.980 --> 01:17:26.520
When you can match the preparation with listening,

1926
01:17:26.520 --> 01:17:28.143
I think, people ask me,

1927
01:17:29.430 --> 01:17:32.850
give me some advice about how to conduct an interview

1928
01:17:32.850 --> 01:17:34.140
or how to do a podcast.

1929
01:17:34.140 --> 01:17:37.980
It's follow your curiosity, there's only one you.

1930
01:17:37.980 --> 01:17:40.500
Be super prepared for what you're gonna do,

1931
01:17:40.500 --> 01:17:41.577
and then show up and listen.

1932
01:17:41.577 --> 01:17:44.580
And I feel so many people are in their own heads

1933
01:17:44.580 --> 01:17:45.960
or thinking,

1934
01:17:45.960 --> 01:17:48.390
in the very narrow context of podcasting,

1935
01:17:48.390 --> 01:17:51.025
so many hosts, they're not listening to their guests,

1936
01:17:51.025 --> 01:17:53.370
they're just waiting to ask the next question,

1937
01:17:53.370 --> 01:17:55.770
or they're just biting their time until they can tell

1938
01:17:55.770 --> 01:17:58.830
their story about their take on something-

1939
01:17:58.830 --> 01:17:59.970
And not just in podcast,

1940
01:17:59.970 --> 01:18:01.680
you see that on television interviews,

1941
01:18:01.680 --> 01:18:02.550
you see it all over.

1942
01:18:02.550 --> 01:18:03.703
And it's terrible, right?

1943
01:18:03.703 --> 01:18:05.670
Yeah, it's not the best interviewing.

1944
01:18:05.670 --> 01:18:08.070
The best interviewing is when you can find a spot

1945
01:18:08.070 --> 01:18:09.000
that you can dive deeper,

1946
01:18:09.000 --> 01:18:12.390
you can find a little rabbit holes, burrow into 'em.

1947
01:18:12.390 --> 01:18:16.070
But also if you're taking that to daily life...

1948
01:18:17.940 --> 01:18:20.220
If taking the listening piece,

1949
01:18:20.220 --> 01:18:21.810
most people are terrible Listeners,

1950
01:18:21.810 --> 01:18:23.430
how would you transfer that?

1951
01:18:23.430 --> 01:18:26.040
I'm less skilled in the real world than I am.

1952
01:18:26.040 --> 01:18:28.156
I don't believe that.

1953
01:18:28.156 --> 01:18:30.120
Yeah, it's about being present, right?

1954
01:18:30.120 --> 01:18:31.770
Are you actually listening to the people

1955
01:18:31.770 --> 01:18:33.870
that you're engaging with during the day?

1956
01:18:33.870 --> 01:18:36.670
And that's definitely

1957
01:18:37.860 --> 01:18:40.230
something that I still need a lot of work on.

1958
01:18:40.230 --> 01:18:43.050
I'm busy and I'm trying to get from one place to the next

1959
01:18:43.050 --> 01:18:45.150
and I don't really wanna sit and listen to

1960
01:18:45.150 --> 01:18:47.550
what somebody's trying to tell me

1961
01:18:47.550 --> 01:18:50.400
when I'm perceiving them to be an obstacle

1962
01:18:50.400 --> 01:18:52.290
to where I'm actually going.

1963
01:18:52.290 --> 01:18:55.030
So I think it's inextricably linked with

1964
01:18:56.520 --> 01:18:58.173
trying to be in the moment.

1965
01:18:59.070 --> 01:19:00.810
And also that's another thing that

1966
01:19:00.810 --> 01:19:02.580
the phone get in the way of, right?

1967
01:19:02.580 --> 01:19:03.930
Good listening skills.

1968
01:19:03.930 --> 01:19:04.763
'Cause we're constantly-

1969
01:19:04.763 --> 01:19:06.390
I listen to my phone really well.

1970
01:19:06.390 --> 01:19:08.970
Yeah, everyone listens to their phone to the tip top.

1971
01:19:08.970 --> 01:19:10.470
Yes.

1972
01:19:10.470 --> 01:19:14.460
But yeah, that idea of over distraction

1973
01:19:14.460 --> 01:19:15.293
gets in the way of it.

1974
01:19:15.293 --> 01:19:16.126
So that's something

1975
01:19:16.126 --> 01:19:18.057
we now have to be especially conscious of.

1976
01:19:18.057 --> 01:19:20.970
And I think we always had to, but good listeners,

1977
01:19:20.970 --> 01:19:23.283
the less you talk, the more you hear more.

1978
01:19:24.120 --> 01:19:26.010
The next thing is consistency,

1979
01:19:26.010 --> 01:19:28.590
and this is just applicable to everything in life as well.

1980
01:19:28.590 --> 01:19:30.480
Whether you're an athlete or professional,

1981
01:19:30.480 --> 01:19:33.360
whatever it is that you're trying to improve at,

1982
01:19:33.360 --> 01:19:37.290
consistency is the most underrated and important piece

1983
01:19:37.290 --> 01:19:39.240
in the whole equation.

1984
01:19:39.240 --> 01:19:41.430
In the context of this podcast,

1985
01:19:41.430 --> 01:19:43.380
consistency has been everything

1986
01:19:43.380 --> 01:19:44.730
just keep moving forward.

1987
01:19:44.730 --> 01:19:46.503
Every week you put up an episode,

1988
01:19:47.880 --> 01:19:49.830
they're not all gonna be home runs,

1989
01:19:49.830 --> 01:19:51.210
just keep moving,

1990
01:19:51.210 --> 01:19:55.260
let go of perfection and create a sustainable model

1991
01:19:55.260 --> 01:19:57.240
that factors in self care

1992
01:19:57.240 --> 01:20:01.830
so that you're in the best position to continue doing

1993
01:20:01.830 --> 01:20:05.370
the thing for as long as you remain curious

1994
01:20:05.370 --> 01:20:06.810
and passionate about it.

1995
01:20:06.810 --> 01:20:09.900
And on the subject of preparing for guests,

1996
01:20:09.900 --> 01:20:12.030
I listen to a lot of podcasts, right?

1997
01:20:12.030 --> 01:20:13.320
If a guest is coming on,

1998
01:20:13.320 --> 01:20:16.590
I'll check out some other podcast appearances they've done

1999
01:20:16.590 --> 01:20:17.880
to get a sense of,

2000
01:20:17.880 --> 01:20:20.160
who they are or some backstory.

2001
01:20:20.160 --> 01:20:24.690
And what I found, not all the time, but

2002
01:20:24.690 --> 01:20:27.270
there's a large percentage of hosts

2003
01:20:27.270 --> 01:20:29.430
who are either ill prepared,

2004
01:20:29.430 --> 01:20:33.330
we talked about earlier or who are not listening,

2005
01:20:33.330 --> 01:20:36.460
just waiting to ask the next question or to tell their story

2006
01:20:37.320 --> 01:20:41.280
or in certain cases, it's great.

2007
01:20:41.280 --> 01:20:44.010
But then I realized that host is no longer podcasting.

2008
01:20:44.010 --> 01:20:46.050
Like this was from a couple years ago and they quit

2009
01:20:46.050 --> 01:20:50.580
or they stopped because they couldn't maintain consistency

2010
01:20:50.580 --> 01:20:52.860
because they couldn't find a way to make it sustainable.

2011
01:20:52.860 --> 01:20:55.140
Like that's my only inference.

2012
01:20:55.140 --> 01:20:57.660
So consistency is super important.

2013
01:20:57.660 --> 01:21:00.810
And that means creating a system that is sustainable.

2014
01:21:00.810 --> 01:21:03.570
So paying attention to how you've set everything up.

2015
01:21:03.570 --> 01:21:06.009
And this is, I've had to learn from my own mistakes,

2016
01:21:06.009 --> 01:21:08.700
starting off, trying to do everything,

2017
01:21:08.700 --> 01:21:11.250
being super perfectionist about everything and then learning

2018
01:21:11.250 --> 01:21:15.660
how to let go and empower team members and,

2019
01:21:15.660 --> 01:21:17.230
create a schedule

2020
01:21:18.349 --> 01:21:23.010
that has allowed us to batch work

2021
01:21:23.010 --> 01:21:26.220
and schedule way in advance so that everybody has

2022
01:21:26.220 --> 01:21:27.630
an enhanced quality of life,

2023
01:21:27.630 --> 01:21:29.670
and then taking time off at the end of the year

2024
01:21:29.670 --> 01:21:32.490
so I can reboot, all of that has been huge,

2025
01:21:32.490 --> 01:21:35.520
so that I can remain as curious and as excited

2026
01:21:35.520 --> 01:21:38.913
about doing this thing 10 years after it's begun.

2027
01:21:39.990 --> 01:21:42.120
I love it, and that's for sure,

2028
01:21:42.120 --> 01:21:45.940
hallmark of anyone in the creative arts consistency

2029
01:21:47.013 --> 01:21:50.246
is paramount because most of the time

2030
01:21:50.246 --> 01:21:51.870
you won't even get anywhere for the first five years.

2031
01:21:51.870 --> 01:21:54.420
So if you give up in that period of time, then-

2032
01:21:54.420 --> 01:21:56.940
Yeah, and on that note, patience, right?

2033
01:21:56.940 --> 01:21:58.560
Yeah, you don't get anywhere in five years.

2034
01:21:58.560 --> 01:22:00.087
Like we hear all this stuff about,

2035
01:22:00.087 --> 01:22:02.610
oh, to change a habit you need 28 days.

2036
01:22:02.610 --> 01:22:03.443
Like, first of all,

2037
01:22:03.443 --> 01:22:05.310
I think it's probably more a hundred days,

2038
01:22:05.310 --> 01:22:08.613
but that aside, if you wanna be good at anything,

2039
01:22:08.613 --> 01:22:10.020
it's gonna take 10 years.

2040
01:22:10.020 --> 01:22:14.460
I think a decade is really the best metric

2041
01:22:14.460 --> 01:22:19.380
that I've seen in order to go from not necessarily beginner,

2042
01:22:19.380 --> 01:22:21.910
but from competency to

2043
01:22:22.770 --> 01:22:26.130
maybe not mastery, but true competency.

2044
01:22:26.130 --> 01:22:28.200
Like 10,000 hours becomes 10 years for you.

2045
01:22:28.200 --> 01:22:29.033
Whatever it is.

2046
01:22:29.033 --> 01:22:31.470
Like, I don't know if you can be that reductive about it,

2047
01:22:31.470 --> 01:22:32.880
but things take time.

2048
01:22:32.880 --> 01:22:35.790
And you can only rush things so much.

2049
01:22:35.790 --> 01:22:38.970
So instead of trying to rush it, just understand,

2050
01:22:38.970 --> 01:22:42.360
this is what you're in for, relax into it,

2051
01:22:42.360 --> 01:22:43.560
build over time.

2052
01:22:43.560 --> 01:22:44.940
Whether you're trying to build a business,

2053
01:22:44.940 --> 01:22:46.261
develop a client base,

2054
01:22:46.261 --> 01:22:49.620
whatever you're trying to get good at

2055
01:22:49.620 --> 01:22:52.080
it's important to think in decades,

2056
01:22:52.080 --> 01:22:53.583
not in days or months or years.

2057
01:22:53.583 --> 01:22:54.670
It's very tough

2058
01:22:55.650 --> 01:22:57.540
when you have this dream and you're young,

2059
01:22:57.540 --> 01:23:02.070
and you don't really see God seems so far away.

2060
01:23:02.070 --> 01:23:02.903
Yeah, I remember-

2061
01:23:02.903 --> 01:23:05.580
I mean, if you're a new lawyer, it's gonna be 10 years

2062
01:23:05.580 --> 01:23:08.430
before you have a book of business and you're kind of

2063
01:23:08.430 --> 01:23:10.860
well regarded as being somewhat expert

2064
01:23:10.860 --> 01:23:13.020
in your specific field.

2065
01:23:13.020 --> 01:23:15.420
I'm sure the same is true if you're a doctor.

2066
01:23:15.420 --> 01:23:20.420
If you're an engineer and certainly, as a podcast.

2067
01:23:20.430 --> 01:23:23.190
We're at 10 years now, when we started, we were small.

2068
01:23:23.190 --> 01:23:24.450
Now we're larger.

2069
01:23:24.450 --> 01:23:28.200
I still have tons to learn, I would not consider myself

2070
01:23:28.200 --> 01:23:29.670
to have mastered this at all.

2071
01:23:29.670 --> 01:23:32.133
Like I try to approach it with a beginner's mind.

2072
01:23:32.970 --> 01:23:36.270
But yeah, it took 10 years to go from where we were

2073
01:23:36.270 --> 01:23:37.680
to where we are today.

2074
01:23:37.680 --> 01:23:38.550
And that's just the deal.

2075
01:23:38.550 --> 01:23:43.230
When I was a swimmer, it took me from age 10 to 20.

2076
01:23:43.230 --> 01:23:46.140
Good, obviously there's some physical development in there,

2077
01:23:46.140 --> 01:23:48.330
but that was a 10 year period.

2078
01:23:48.330 --> 01:23:53.182
And with podcasting, it's been from 2012 to 2022.

2079
01:23:53.182 --> 01:23:54.900
You know, it's funny,

2080
01:23:54.900 --> 01:23:57.450
in the end of 2004, beginning 2005,

2081
